## LOAD (binding; the MPS heavy-slot convention, RELAYED from the B546 and B547 kits)
8 cores. Heavy = `npm ci`, `npm test`, `vitest run`, `npm run lint`, `npm run build`, a mutant matrix, the full CI line. **Start a heavy run only while `uptime` load1 is below 8** at two reads 4 s apart (not independent: say so), recorded beside the result; at 8 or over poll every 20 s, log each wait, and do the light work meanwhile (C1, the reading half of C2, C7, C8, C9). **Then take ONE slot atomically:** `mkdir '<project root>/.tools/heavy-slot-1' 2>/dev/null || mkdir '<project root>/.tools/heavy-slot-2' 2>/dev/null || mkdir '<project root>/.tools/heavy-slot-3'`, write `B553 <start time> <label>` into its `holder`; read every other `heavy-slot-*/holder` first (G68 will hold one). A slot is taken when its DIRECTORY exists. A holder that looks stale: report it, never remove it. All taken: wait 3 minutes and retry. At most ONE slot, one heavy job at a time (a mutant matrix is one job; a filtered `vitest run` per mutant is a heavy run inside the held slot; **re-read load1 before EACH run inside the slot, not only before the first**: the round-1 builder's lapse was exactly this; **the five full-suite timing runs each need their own fresh read**, and a timing run started at load1 over 8 is not a timing run: discard it, say so, repeat); at the run's end (pass, fail or abort) remove ONLY your own slot (`holder` + `rmdir`, same block). Write your OWN wrapper in `.tools/b553/` (never copy or run another seat's script). Before your last line `ls '<project root>/.tools/' | /usr/bin/grep heavy-slot` shows no holder naming B553. Never kill a process by name; only your own ancestry. NO artificial load. `npm ci` only inside YOUR worktrees' `web/` (head, mut, prev). Record `node --version`, `npm --version`, `npx vitest --version`.

